Provenance and a note on the license's name
-------------------------------------------

The code in this repository is the reference implementation published in
RFC 6234, "US Secure Hash Algorithms (SHA and SHA-based HMAC and HKDF)",
Section 8:

    https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6234#section-8

That makes it a Code Component of an IETF Document. Under Section 4.e of the
IETF Trust Legal Provisions (TLP), Code Components are licensed under the BSD
license reproduced above -- the same notice the code itself carries, in the
header of include/sha.h:

    https://trustee.ietf.org/documents/trust-legal-provisions/

RFC 6234 (May 2011) refers to this license as the "Simplified BSD License".
That name is misleading, and the IETF Trust has since said so explicitly: the
TLP "erroneously called the [license] the 'Simplified BSD License' rather than
the 'Revised BSD License', and many documents that refer to these provisions
copied the erroneous name. The IETF Trust corrected the error on September 21,
2021. The license text itself was always that of the Revised BSD License and
has not changed."

This matters because "Simplified BSD" conventionally means the *two*-clause
BSD license, whereas the text above has *three* clauses -- it includes the
"Neither the name of Internet Society, IETF or IETF Trust ... may be used to
endorse or promote" clause. The correct SPDX identifier is therefore
BSD-3-Clause, not BSD-2-Clause.

One further clarification, since it is a common misreading: the TLP does place
restrictions on modifying and redistributing the text of an RFC *document*.
Those restrictions apply to the document, not to Code Components extracted
from it. This code is covered solely by the BSD license above, so it may be
used, modified and redistributed on those terms like any other open source
code.
